enant end-to-end across the split topology → E005/branch ACs.\n- Single-tenant regression, fail-closed → E003 ACs.\n\nThe effort estimate's \"integration testing 1-2 sprints\" would then be distributed as ACs. Hmm. But a reviewer might flag \"strategy budgets 1-2 sprints of integration testing; where is it?\" — distributed ACs are a legitimate answer, but an explicit epic is more traceable. \n\nI'll keep E006 but check the validator's priority rule first. If the validator flags E005(P1)→E006(P0), I can set E006=P1? No... E006 verifies P0. Hmm, actually, is E006 really P0? Its HLR coverage: it doesn't \"cover\" HLRs per se — it validates them. In the traceability matrix, HLRs map to E003/E004/E005 primarily; E006 could map to... hmm, actually if HLRs are already covered by E003/E004/E005, E006 covers none → is it orphaned scope? Its mandate comes from the effort estimate (integration testing work stream) and strategy ACs. In the matrix I could list E006 as secondary coverage.\n\nOK let me just read the validator and frontmatter schema now, then finalize.\n\nAlso — should E006 depend on E005 at all? If E006's scope = \"end-to-end multi-tenant validation across the split-server topology\", it needs the operator topology (E005) and routing (branch). Hmm, routing! If E006 validates \"split-server routing\" (explicitly in the effort estimate), it depends on routing which is branch-dependent → per the branch rules, \"if it depends on a specific branch's work, it belongs inside that branch\". But it depends on routing-in-general (whichever branch)... The rule says depend on the Investigation (E002), not branch outcomes. So E006 deps: E003, E004, E005, E002? That makes E002 (P1 INV) block E006 (P0?) — same inflation problem.\n\nSimplify E006: scope = multi-tenant workspace scenarios, backwards compatibility, single-tenant regression, fail-closed edge cases, operator split-server topology reconciliation (ACs 1-6 as applicable). Deps: E003, E004, E005. Routing validation lives