{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-ac3d5051-081f-4f5c-9a6c-f417c89049f2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a2a689796cdf92c5","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-k_v0du9z/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-E005.md","content":"# Operator split-server topology for the dedicated artifacts-only instance\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic delivers the MLflow operator's split-server topology: the operator deploys a dedicated artifacts-only MLflow instance as a separate Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute, and configures the main tracking deployment to route artifact traffic to that dedicated instance. The main tracking server continues to handle experiment tracking, model registry, and prompt registry, while the artifacts-only instance handles artifact serving with workspace validation. Per the Staff Engineer input, this is a split-server topology, not a single-deployment configuration toggle. The operator already uses a Helm-in-operator pattern (MLflow CR spec converted to Helm values, rendered through embedded chart templates, applied via Server-Side Apply), and this epic extends that pattern with the artifacts-only resources. The mechanism for routing artifact traffic from the main tracking deployment follows the outcome of the routing-approach investigation (gateway/HTTPRoute-level routing is the default design; if the investigation determines the tracking server needs a delegation configuration change, the routing portion of this epic is rewritten around that approach and the conditional tracking-server delegation work is activated).\n\n## Scope\n\n- New Helm chart templates for the dedicated artifacts-only Deployment, Service, and HTTPRoute, rendered and applied through the operator's existing Helm-in-operator / Server-Side Apply pattern.\n- Configure the main tracking deployment to route artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance, per the routing mechanism determined by the routing-approach investigation (default: gateway/HTTPRoute-level routing).\n- Propagate workspace configuration (`--enable-workspaces`, `--workspace-store-uri`, `--app-name kubernetes-auth`) to the artifacts-only instance using the same configuration mechanisms the operator already uses for the main tracking deployment, along with `--artifacts-only` and `--serve-artifacts`.\n- Create Gateway API HTTPRoute resources conditionally based on runtime CRD discovery, consistent with the operator's existing pattern for optional dependencies.\n- Operator validation that rejects configurations where the artifacts-only instance would be deployed without the workspace flags (per the strategy's risk mitigation: misconfiguration must not be able to result in artifact requests bypassing workspace validation).\n- Keep the topology declarative: users configure a single MLflow CR rather than managing individual Deployments, Services, or HTTPRoutes.\n- Deploy the kube-rbac-proxy sidecar on the artifacts-only instance identically to the main tracking deployment (authentication layer unchanged).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given an MLflow CR with split-server artifact serving enabled, when the operator reconciles the CR, then the operator creates a dedicated artifacts-only Deployment, Service, and HTTPRoute separate from the main tracking Deployment, measured by the presence of distinct Deployment resources with the correct flag combinations.\n- The dedicated artifacts-only Deployment runs with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes:// --app-name kubernetes-auth --serve-artifacts`, measured by inspecting the container command in the rendered Deployment spec.\n- The main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance via the mechanism selected by the routing-approach investigation, and artifact traffic cannot reach artifact content without passing through the artifacts-only instance's workspace validation.\n- HTTPRoute resources for the artifacts-only instance are created only when the Gateway API CRD is discovered at runtime (existing conditional pattern preserved), and the operator reconciles successfully without Gateway API present.\n- The operator rejects (with a clear status condition or error) MLflow CR configurations that would deploy the artifacts-only instance without the workspace flags.\n- The split topology is managed declaratively from a single MLflow CR: no manual creation of the artifacts-only Deployment, Service, or HTTPRoute is required.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- **HLR-5** (P1): operator deploys a dedicated artifacts-only instance as a separate Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute — delivered here.\n- **HLR-6** (P1): main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance — delivered here (routing mechanism per the routing-approach investigation; if the tracking server requires a delegation configuration change, the conditional tracking-server delegation work delivers that part).\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":88523,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-94bc8ba55f0f6fae","agent_id":"a9ea81eb8dd2822c4","session_id":"4ef6c81f-5e30-4fc1-86dc-31c77ed3b7ca","uuid":"020701be-431a-44df-8e13-59290a2ab6b2","timestamp":"2026-10-26T10:40:54.415Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8009"}
