{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-3ceb0793-c46c-456c-9118-74aa3d5479d1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-8fc427250fdb7d9a","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-k_v0du9z/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-E006.md","content":"# End-to-end multi-tenant validation of workspace-aware artifact serving\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic delivers the automated end-to-end validation of workspace-aware artifact serving across the server and auth plugin components, covering the strategy's multi-tenant acceptance scenarios: workspace member access, cross-workspace denial, fail-closed behavior when the workspace store is unreachable, backwards compatibility, and single-tenant regression. The strategy's effort estimate budgets 1-2 sprints for integration testing (multi-tenant workspace scenarios, backwards compatibility, single-tenant regression, fail-closed edge cases); this epic is that work stream, producing a reusable scenario suite that runs against a realistic multi-tenant environment with the kubernetes-auth authentication, the `kubernetes://` workspace store, and S3 artifact storage. It owns the strategy's end-to-end acceptance criteria 1-4 (the operator topology's reconciliation criteria are carried by the operator work, and split-server routing validation is carried by the operator topology and routing work).\n\n## Scope\n\n- An automated multi-tenant scenario suite covering: a workspace member successfully retrieving an artifact from their workspace's experiment (HTTP 200 with correct content); a member of one workspace denied an artifact belonging to another workspace's experiment (HTTP 403 with insufficient-permissions error); the workspace-aware artifacts-only instance denying requests when the workspace store is unreachable (HTTP 503, fail-closed); and artifacts-only serving without workspace flags preserving current behavior (HTTP 200 regardless of workspace membership).\n- Single-tenant regression scenarios: deployments that do not set `--enable-workspaces` retain current artifact-serving behavior.\n- Backwards-compatibility scenarios: omitting either flag (`--artifacts-only` without `--enable-workspaces`, or `--enable-workspaces` without `--artifacts-only`) preserves current behavior.\n- Fail-closed edge cases: transient workspace store unavailability, invalid or missing workspace context in requests (denied, not served).\n- The suite runs the artifacts-only server with the combined flag configuration against the kubernetes-auth plugin, the `kubernetes://` workspace store, and the S3 artifact store (the standard RHOAI configuration), exercising the plugin's workspace resolution end-to-end.\n- Results reported per scenario with pass/fail status, usable as release evidence for the multi-tenant isolation requirement.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The scenario suite passes end-to-end: a member of workspace A retrieving an artifact of an experiment in workspace A through the workspace-aware artifacts-only instance receives HTTP 200 with the correct artifact content.\n- The scenario suite passes the denial case: a member of workspace A but not workspace B requesting an artifact of an experiment in workspace B receives HTTP 403 with an error indicating insufficient workspace permissions.\n- The scenario suite passes the fail-closed case: with the workspace store (Kubernetes API) unreachable, artifact requests are denied with HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable.\n- The scenario suite passes the no-flags case: with `--artifacts-only` set without `--enable-workspaces`, any authenticated user's artifact request returns HTTP 200 regardless of workspace membership (current behavior preserved).\n- Single-tenant regression and backwards-compatibility scenarios pass: deployments without workspace flags exhibit current artifact-serving behavior.\n- All scenarios execute against an environment using the kubernetes-auth authentication plugin, the `kubernetes://` workspace store, and the S3 artifact store, and produce a per-scenario pass/fail report.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- **HLR-1, HLR-2, HLR-3, HLR-4** (P0): this epic provides the end-to-end verification of these requirements — the strategy acceptance criteria 1-4 are owned here as measurable scenarios.\n- **HLR-8** (P2): the denial scenarios verify that workspace access denials are recorded in the server logs.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":90128,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-94bc8ba55f0f6fae","agent_id":"a9ea81eb8dd2822c4","session_id":"4ef6c81f-5e30-4fc1-86dc-31c77ed3b7ca","uuid":"a604029d-a290-494c-bf02-624635c00eb0","timestamp":"2026-10-26T10:41:08.122Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8009"}
