{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-83c57f80877a5b44","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8009-E003\n3\ttitle: Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n5\tcomponent: MLflow\n6\tteam: MLflow Team\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-8009-E001\n11\t- RHAISTRAT-8009-E004\n12\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8009-E001\n13\tgate_failure_impact:\n14\t  action: rewrite\n15\t  fallback_approach: If the investigation finds the workspace provider cannot be initialized\n16\t    independently of tracking stores, the server initialization sequence and validation\n17\t    approach are redesigned per its findings (e.g., a slimmed initialization path\n18\t    or a plugin refactor); the epic is rewritten, not removed.\n19\tai_signals:\n20\t  change_specificity: 1\n21\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n22\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n23\t  existing_foundation: 1\n24\t  open_questions: 0\n25\t  external_dependency: 1\n26\t  human_process_gates: 0\n27\t  repo_access: 1\n28\t  architecture_claims: 1\n29\timplementation_type: null\n30\tinvestigation_signals: null\n31\tai_implementability: High\n32\tai_implementability_score: 7\n33\tjira_key: null\n34\tbranch: null\n35\t---\n36\t# Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n37\t\n38\t## Description\n39\t\n40\tThis epic makes `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` in the MLflow tracking server. When both flags are set, the server requires an explicit `--workspace-store-uri`, initializes only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests, and leaves tracking stores, model registry stores, and non-artifact API endpoints uninitialized/disabled. Each artifact request is resolved to its parent experiment, the experiment's workspace assignment is looked up via the workspace store, and authorization is delegated to the existing auth plugin infrastructure; requests failing workspace validation are denied with HTTP 403. The design is fail-closed: if the workspace store is unreachable, artifact requests are denied rather than served without validation. No new server flag is introduced — per the Staff Engineer input, the workspace-aware behavior emerges from combining existing flags. This epic delivers the P0 core of the strategy.\n41\t\n42\t## Scope\n43\t\n44\t- Make `--artifacts-only` and `--enable-workspaces` composable: when both are set, initialize only the workspace provider (per the initialization-coupling investigation findings), keeping tracking and model registry stores uninitialized and non-artifact endpoints disabled.\n45\t- Require an explicit `--workspace-store-uri` when both flags are set; fail to start with a clear error if it is absent (per the strategy's dependency table).\n46\t- Add workspace validation to artifact request handling in the artifacts-only server: resolve the artifact path to its parent experiment (artifacts stored under experiment-scoped paths, e.g. `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`), look up the experiment's workspace assignment via the workspace store, and delegate authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure (`--app-name kubernetes-auth`), without dictating Kubernetes API mechanics.\n47\t- Deny requests from users without workspace membership with HTTP 403 and an error indicating insufficient workspace permissions.\n48\t- Implement fail-closed behavior: if the workspace-aware mode is active but the workspace store is unreachable, deny artifact requests (HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable) rather than serving without validation.\n49\t- Preserve current behavior when either flag is absent: `--artifacts-only` without `--enable-workspaces` serves artifacts without workspace validation, and single-tenant deployments are unaffected. The both-flags condition is itself the activation gate — omitting either flag preserves current behavior.\n50\t- Emit audit log entries for workspace access denials in artifact serving through server logs (requesting identity, artifact path/experiment, and decision).\n51\t- Scope artifact path resolution to the S3 artifact store (the standard RHOAI configuration) with its well-defined `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/` structure, and document limitations for non-standard artifact roots.\n52\t\n53\tRisks and notes (from the strategy):\n54\t\n55\t- **Performance**: workspace resolution adds a workspace store lookup and auth plugin validation per artifact request; no latency target is specified. If measured overhead exceeds the acceptable threshold (a pending PM/Engineering decision), implement short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions, consistent with caching patterns already used in the tracking server's auth path. Whether caching is required at launch or deferred does not change this epic's structure.\n56\t- This epic builds on the auth plugin workspace resolution functioning in the artifacts-only context (the plugin work) and on the initialization-coupling investigation findings; its initialization sequence follows whatever path that work establishes.\n57\t\n58\t## Acceptance Criteria\n59\t\n60\t- A server started with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes://` initializes the workspace provider without initializing tracking or model registry stores, and non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled.\n61\t- A server started with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces` but without `--workspace-store-uri` fails to start with a clear error message.\n62\t- A user who is a member of workspace A requesting, through the artifacts-only instance, an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace A receives HTTP 200 with the correct artifact content.\n63\t- A user who is a member of workspace A but not workspace B requesting an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace B receives HTTP 403 with an error message indicating insufficient workspace permissions.\n64\t- With the workspace-aware artifacts-only instance running and the workspace store (Kubernetes API) unreachable, artifact requests are denied with HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable (fail-closed).\n65\t- A server started with `--artifacts-only` without `--enable-workspaces` serves artifacts to any authenticated user without workspace validation — current behavior preserved, measured by HTTP 200 regardless of workspace membership.\n66\t- Workspace access denials in artifact serving are recorded in the server logs with requesting identity, artifact path or experiment, and the denial decision.\n67\t- Artifact path resolution is implemented for the standard S3 artifact store path structure, and limitations for non-standard artifact roots are documented.\n68\t\n69\t## HLR Traceability\n70\t\n71\t- **HLR-1** (P0): `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri` — delivered here.\n72\t- **HLR-2** (P0): artifact requests validated against workspace membership, non-members denied HTTP 403 — delivered here.\n73\t- **HLR-3** (P0): fail-closed when the workspace store is unreachable — delivered here.\n74\t- **HLR-4** (P0): single-tenant and no-workspace-flag deployments retain current behavior — delivered here.\n75\t- **HLR-8** (P2, bundled): audit logging for workspace access denials through server logs — bundled with the P0 validation work because the audit entries are emitted in the same denial path the P0 requirements implement; splitting would double-touch the same request-handling surface.\n76\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9cc6d5e2a099eed6","agent_id":"a1d712262287d63fb","session_id":"4ef6c81f-5e30-4fc1-86dc-31c77ed3b7ca","uuid":"bc3dd896-db66-4383-9bf3-ac32197ba8fb","timestamp":"2026-10-26T10:43:46.976Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-83c57f80877a5b44","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8009 decomposition"}
