E005's routing approach (`rewrite` impact). All Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts (server capability, plugin changes, operator topology, test suite). `gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact` metadata is complete and correct on all four gated epics; E006's dependencies on E003/E004 are Implementation-to-Implementation edges that correctly carry no `gated_by`. The branch epic's `remove` action with a fallback approach is exactly right for outcome-A-removes-the-epic.\n43\t\n44\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n45\t\n46\tType-appropriate signal sets throughout: E001/E002 carry `investigation_signals` (5-signal rubric), all Implementation epics and the branch epic carry `ai_signals` (9-signal rubric); no cross-contamination. Every frontmatter value matches its rationale row in the corresponding ai-signals file. The `architecture_claims: 1` scores are properly calibrated against `.context`: the strategy makes architecture claims (Helm-in-operator pattern, conditional CRD discovery, plugin registration via `mlflow.workspace_provider`), the cited files (`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/mlflow.md`, `mlflow-operator.md`) exist, and each rationale cites the specific corroborating content — including honest handling of the plugin's missing dedicated context file on E004. Signal values that could be challenged are each backed by explicit reasoning: E004's `external_dependency: 0` (PyPI version-bump coordination, not upstream contribution), E006's `pattern_precedent: 0` (new scenario suite), E001's `local_runnability: 0` (workspace store needs a live Kubernetes API). One calibration inconsistency noted as a minor issue: E003 scores `open_questions: 0` while E004 and E005 score `-1` under structurally identical gating (all three are gated with `rewrite` impact by an investigation). E003's rationale — scope pinned by exact flags and behaviors, the gate affecting only the initialization approach — is defensible, so this does not contradict the rubric, but the calibration across the three gated epics is not uniform. Arithmetic and thresholds not checked, per instructions.\n47\t\n48\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n49\t\n50\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy: E003's ACs mirror the strategy's acceptance criteria 1-4 with exact HTTP status codes and flag combinations; E005 carries strategy ACs 5-6; E006 owns ACs 1-4 as measurable end-to-end scenarios; E001/E002 ACs require the findings/decision document to state bounded outcomes with cited evidence. No rule-mandated AC is applicable and missing: no replacement epic exists (backwards compatibility is nonetheless covered by explicit preserve-behavior ACs on E003, E005, and the branch epic), no konflux chain exists (both repos already have pipelines), and no docs-authoring epic exists. Scanned every AC for externally-controlled preconditions misfiled as ACs — none found; the E006 environment AC (\"scenarios execute against an environment using...\") is a condition on what the epic itself builds and verifies, and the strategy's lab/cluster needs are handled as risks and health warnings, not ACs. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID — grep confirms epic IDs appear only in frontmatter fields; bodies use descriptive phrases (\"the routing-approach investigation\", \"the plugin work\").\n51\t\n52\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n53\t\n54\tFull scope coverage: server flag composition and workspace validation (E003), plugin resolution (E004), operator split-server topology and routing (E005 plus conditional branch), integration testing (E006), and both genuine unknowns as investigations (E001, E002). All six strategy acceptance criteria are allocated (1-4 to E003/E006, 5-6 to E005). Strategy-level context is carried forward, not dropped: all four risks appear as epic scope/ACs or recorded risks (path resolution scoped to S3 with documented limitations; plugin coupling as E001/E004; split-topology misconfiguration as E005's operator-validation AC and the no-bypass routing AC; per-request latency and caching as an E003 risk plus a health warning); all five \"needs validation\" assumptions map to E001/E002 investigation questions or E005's work; all four open questions are either resolved by an investigation (routing, plugin initialization) or flagged for human verification with impact analysis (latency target, audit telemetry channel). Out-of-scope items are correctly excluded. The conditional decomposition covers both bounded outcomes (A: main plan as written; B: branch epic plus E005 rewrite), with HLR-6's conditional coverage recorded in the matrix. Docs-authoring check: the strategy's Prerequisites list shows Documentation Support as \"Pending human review\", not \"Yes\", so no Rule 11 docs-authoring epic is required — and the summary explicitly flags the condition under which one would be added rather than silently dropping it.\n55\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8be3096eb3fe156a","agent_id":"a7e5e5b79fa9dfcad","session_id":"4ef6c81f-5e30-4fc1-86dc-31c77ed3b7ca","uuid":"95f9b072-f45c-416e-b90e-d6953923534d","timestamp":"2026-10-26T10:47:50.620Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-80c3bf555411e2fc","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8009 decomposition"}
