{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9d8499dce80f3e75","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n3\tepic_count: 6\n4\tcritical_path_length: 4\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-8009 — Workspace-aware artifact serving for multi-tenant MLflow deployments\n11\t\n12\t**Triage**: full decomposition. The strategy is M-L (4-7 sprints per its own effort estimate), spans three strategy components (MLflow, MLflow Operator, mlflow-kubernetes-plugins) with eight HLRs across P0/P1/P2, and contains genuine unknowns (plugin initialization coupling, routing approach) whose answers change what downstream epics do. Neither Step 0 escape applies.\n13\t\n14\t**Staff Engineer / SME Input precedence**: the strategy body already incorporates the Staff Engineer corrections; this decomposition follows them and logs the overrides below (see \"Staff Engineer Input Overrides\").\n15\t\n16\t## Epic List\n17\t\n18\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n19\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n20\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E001 | Investigate workspace-store and auth-plugin initialization in the artifacts-only server context | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n21\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E002 | Investigate artifact traffic routing approach for the split-server topology | Investigation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n22\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n23\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | Auth plugin workspace resolution in the artifacts-only server context | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 | Operator split-server topology for the dedicated artifacts-only instance | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E006 | End-to-end multi-tenant validation of workspace-aware artifact serving | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n26\t\n27\tOne conditional branch epic additionally exists (RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E006, tracking-server artifact delegation — only if the routing investigation resolves to outcome B); see \"Conditional Decomposition\". `epic_count` counts the six main-plan epics.\n28\t\n29\t## Dependency DAG\n30\t\n31\tArrows point from dependency to dependent (`E001 --> E003` means E001 must complete before E003 starts).\n32\t\n33\t```mermaid\n34\tgraph TD\n35\t    E001 --> E004\n36\t    E001 --> E003\n37\t    E004 --> E003\n38\t    E002 --> E005\n39\t    E003 --> E005\n40\t    E003 --> E006\n41\t    E004 --> E006\n42\t```\n43\t\n44\t- E001 (Investigation: initialization coupling) and E002 (Investigation: routing approach) are independent and start in parallel.\n45\t- Critical path: E001 → E004 → E003 → E005 (length 4).\n46\t- Branch epic RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E006 depends only on E002 (its gate) and is excluded from the main-plan critical path.\n47\t\n48\t## DAG Justification\n49\t\n50\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n51\t|------|------|-----------|\n52\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | Whether the workspace provider can be initialized without tracking/registry stores determines the server's initialization sequence for workspace-aware artifacts-only mode; true gate with `rewrite` impact. |\n53\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The same investigation determines whether the plugin epic is verification-only or requires refactoring workspace resolution into an independent initialization path; true gate with `rewrite` impact. |\n54\t| E004 → E003 | Rule 13 (library → consumer) | The server's workspace validation builds against the plugin's workspace-resolution capability functioning in the artifacts-only context; the plugin is the library the server code calls. |\n55\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The routing-approach investigation determines how the main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic (HTTPRoute design vs server-side delegation) — a scope/approach gate with `rewrite` impact on the operator epic. |\n56\t| E003 → E005 | Rule 12 (framework → consumer) | The operator topology deploys a dedicated artifacts-only instance whose container command requires the server's workspace-aware artifacts-only capability; the operator epic's own acceptance criteria (the instance runs with the combined flags) validate against that capability. This is a capability dependency, not a configuration reference such as an image digest. |\n57\t| E003 → E006 | Rule 12 (framework → consumer) | The end-to-end validation suite exercises the delivered server capability; it cannot pass before the capability exists. |\n58\t| E004 → E006 | Rule 12 (framework → consumer) | The validation suite exercises the plugin's workspace resolution in the artifacts-only context; same reasoning as above. |\n59\t\n60\tNotable non-edges:\n61\t\n62\t- **E001 ∥ E002**: the two investigations resolve unrelated unknowns (initialization coupling vs routing design) and run in parallel.\n63\t- **E002 ↛ E003**: the routing investigation does not gate the server-side flag composition and validation work — that work is identical under either routing outcome.\n64\t- **E005 is not blocked by E004 directly**: the plugin dependency reaches E005 transitively through E003.\n65\t- **E006 does not depend on E005**: the P0 end-to-end multi-tenant scenarios (strategy ACs 1-4) exercise the artifacts-only server directly and do not require the operator topology; operator-topology reconciliation checks (strategy ACs 5-6) are acceptance criteria on E005, and split-server routing validation is carried by E005 and the conditional branch epic. This also keeps the P1 operator work off the P0 critical closure.\n66\t\n67\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n68\t\n69\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement | Epic | Notes |\n70\t|-----|----------|-------------|------|-------|\n71\t| HLR-1 | P0 | `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri` for workspace-validated artifact serving | RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | Core server flag-composition work; investigation E001 gates the initialization approach. |\n72\t| HLR-2 | P0 | Artifact requests validated against workspace membership; non-members denied HTTP 403 | RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | Validation implemented in the server's artifact request handling, delegating to the auth plugin (E004 enables; E006 verifies end-to-end). |\n73\t| HLR-3 | P0 | Fail-closed behavior when the workspace store is unreachable | RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | Deny rather than serve without validation; verified in E006. |\n74\t| HLR-4 | P0 | Single-tenant deployments and configurations without `--enable-workspaces` retain current behavior | RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | Backwards-compatibility acceptance criteria; regression-verified in E006. |\n75\t| HLR-5 | P1 | Operator deploys dedicated artifacts-only instance as separate Deployment with own Service and HTTPRoute | RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 | Operator split-server topology; strategy ACs 5-6 allocated here. |\n76\t| HLR-6 | P1 | Main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance | RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 (+ RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E006 conditionally) | Main plan assumes gateway/HTTPRoute-level routing per the strategy's technical approach; if the routing investigation (E002) resolves to outcome B, the server-side delegation epic RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E006 is activated and E005's routing portion rewritten. |\n77\t| HLR-7 | P1 | Auth plugin workspace resolution functions in artifacts-only context without tracking store initialization | RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | E004 is P0 by priority inheritance: it transitively enables the P0 workspace-validation requirement (HLR-2) and blocks the P0 server epic. |\n78\t| HLR-8 | P2 | Audit logging for workspace access denials in artifact serving through server logs | RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 (bundled) | Bundled with the P0 work: the audit entries are emitted in the same workspace-validation denial path (the 403 handler) that HLR-2/HLR-3 implement — a split would double-touch the same request-handling surface and build the same code path twice. The pending telemetry-channel decision is recorded as a health warning. |\n79\t\n80\tAll eight HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. E006 provides verification coverage of HLR-1 through HLR-4 and HLR-8 (it owns the strategy acceptance criteria 1-4 as end-to-end scenarios).\n81\t\n82\t## Staff Engineer Input Overrides\n83\t\n84\tPer Step 1.5, the Staff Engineer / SME Input takes precedence over the AI-generated technical approach. Three overrides are logged for traceability:\n85\t\n86\t1. **No new flag.** The original STRAT proposed a `--artifacts-only-workspace-aware` flag and a `spec.artifactsOnly.workspaceAware` CR field; the Staff Engineer directed combining the existing `--artifacts-only` and `--enable-workspaces` flags instead. This decomposition contains no epic for a new server flag or new CR field — E003's scope is flag composition, and E005 propagates existing flags only.\n87\t2. **Operator work is a split-server topology, not a config toggle.** E005's scope is the dedicated artifacts-only Deployment + Service + HTTPRoute plus artifact traffic routing, per the Staff Engineer correction.\n88\t3. **SSAR detail removed from strategy level.** The epic scopes state that workspace authorization reuses the existing auth plugin infrastructure without dictating Kubernetes API mechanics (no SelfSubjectAccessReview prescription); the kube-rbac-proxy sidecar and `--app-name kubernetes-auth` setup are unchanged.\n89\t\n90\t## Component Mapping\n91\t\n92\t- The canonical component list (`.context/rhai-components.txt`) contains `MLflow` but not `MLflow Operator` or `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins`. Every epic therefore carries `component: MLflow` (the closest canonical name — `submit.py` strips unknown names). The per-epic repo spans are: E003, E006, and BRANCH-B-E006 → mlflow server repo; E004 → mlflow-kubernetes-plugins repo; E005 → mlflow-operator repo. E001 spans the mlflow and mlflow-kubernetes-plugins repos (most-relevant canonical name: MLflow).\n93\t- **mlflow-kubernetes-plugins has no dedicated architecture-context file**, but it is not a new component: the strategy's dependency table declares it internal/existing, and `mlflow.md` documents it as the packaged `kubernetes-auth` plugin (v1.5.0) consumed by the MLflow image. No onboarding chain (repo-onboarding / license-validation / konflux-onboarding) is generated for it; see Health Warnings.\n94\t- All three strategy components are active (code changes); there are no passive components and no new components requiring provisioning. MLflow and mlflow-operator both already have Konflux build pipelines per their architecture-context files, so no `konflux-onboarding` epics are generated.\n95\t\n96\t## Health Warnings\n97\t\n98\t- **Planning note (epic size)**: the operator split-server topology epic (E005) is ~2-3 sprints by the strategy's own effort estimate — above the ~2-week epic-size guideline. Kept as one logical change: deploying the dedicated artifacts-only instance and routing artifact traffic to it are one Helm-in-operator topology (the routing design is inseparable from the instance's Service/HTTPRoute design, and the routing-approach investigation covers both); a split would fragment the chart templates and duplicate the routing design work. **Re-split trigger**: re-split this epic if the topology work exceeds 3 sprints of effort, or if the routing investigation resolves to an approach that doubles the surface (server-side delegation requiring separate operator wiring) — in that case split the artifact-traffic-routing work out as its own epic alongside the conditional tracking-server delegation epic.\n99\t- **Component mapping**: `MLflow Operator` and `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` are not in the canonical RHAI Jira component list; all epics carry `MLflow`. Reviewers should read the epic descriptions for the actual target repo.\n100\t- **mlflow-kubernetes-plugins architecture context gap**: no dedicated architecture-context file exists for the plugin. It is treated as in-platform (internal, exists; consumed by the MLflow image per `mlflow.md`). If the plugin's source repo turns out to require new midstream onboarding (fork under opendatahub-io, license scan, Konflux pipeline), add the standard onboarding chain as a follow-up — this decomposition deliberately does not generate it on the strength of the strategy's \"internal, exists\" declaration.\n101\t- **Audit telemetry channel (open question, PM/Security)**: HLR-8 pins audit logging to server logs, and it is implemented as bundled acceptance criteria on E003. The open question — whether workspace access denials should additionally generate audit events through OTLP or the Kubernetes audit log — does not change which epics exist or what the P2 deliverable is (server-log audit entries are implemented either way; an affirmative PM/Security decision would add a follow-up epic expanding the channel). Flagged for human verification rather than downgraded silently.\n102\t- **Latency overhead / caching (open question, PM/Engineering)**: no per-request latency target is specified. The mitigation (short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions, consistent with existing caching patterns in the tracking server's auth path) is recorded as a risk on E003; whether caching is required at launch or deferred is a PM/Engineering decision that does not change the epic structure. Flagged for human verification.\n103\t- **Artifact path resolution scope**: per the strategy's risk mitigation, initial implementation is scoped to the S3 artifact store (standard RHOAI configuration) with the `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/` path structure; limitations for non-standard artifact roots are documented as an E003 acceptance criterion.\n104\t- **Strategy-level process gates**: Architecture Review, ODH/RHOAI Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, Accelerator/Package Support, Documentation Support, UXD Support, and Performance Team Support are all \"Pending human review\" in the strategy. These are strategy-level gates, not epic-level implementation gates (both target repos already have Konflux pipelines), so no epic carries a human-approval acceptance criterion; flagged here for human follow-up.\n105\t- **No documentation epic**: Documentation Support is pending human review (not committed), and the strategy contains no documentation HLR; incidental doc touch-ups fall out of the implementation epics. If documentation support is later confirmed as substantive content authoring, add a `docs-authoring` epic blocked by all Implementation epics.\n106\t\n107\t## Conditional Decomposition\n108\t\n109\tThe routing-approach investigation (E002) has two bounded outcomes that change downstream structure:\n110\t\n111\t- **Outcome A — gateway-level routing suffices**: HTTPRoute rules direct artifact traffic from the main tracking deployment's ingress to the dedicated artifacts-only instance; no tracking-server change is needed. The main plan as written covers this: E005 implements routing at the gateway level per the strategy's technical approach. No additional epic.\n112\t- **Outcome B — tracking-server delegation required**: the tracking server needs a configuration change to delegate artifact operations to the dedicated instance. E005's routing portion is rewritten around server-side delegation, and the conditional epic **RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E006** (Tracking-server artifact operation delegation to the dedicated artifacts-only instance, Implementation, P1, MLflow Team) is activated. Its `gate_failure_impact.action` is `remove`: if outcome A holds, the epic does not exist.\n113\t\n114\tThe branch epic is a plan document attached to the gating investigation, not a standalone Jira issue; no main-plan epic depends on it (per the branch rules, work depending on the routing outcome depends on E002 itself).\n115\t\n116\t## Tiered Delivery\n117\t\n118\tNot applicable. The strategy's dependency table lists only internal, existing dependencies (workspace store, auth plugin, Gateway API, kube-rbac-proxy sidecar); there is no external dependency with uncertain timing, so no Tier 1 / Tier 2 split is needed. All work is within the MLflow Team's components.\n119\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ba2b7d2cee52897d","agent_id":"aaf7aa26bf677378d","session_id":"4ef6c81f-5e30-4fc1-86dc-31c77ed3b7ca","uuid":"b41c9ad7-8343-49fc-9684-563934b1227d","timestamp":"2026-10-26T10:48:59.883Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9d8499dce80f3e75","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8009"}
